     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** Ibuprofen 1600 mg four times daily comes to 6,400 mg/day. That is twice the usual maximum of 3,200 mg/day for adults under supervision. A single 1600 mg dose is also double the typical maximum single dose of 800 mg. This level raises the risk of GI bleeding, kidney injury, and cardiovascular events, even in a patient with normal kidney and liver function.\n\n2. **You're not a clinician.** Medication orders need to come from a licensed prescriber, or be verified by one, and I can't confirm that here.\n\nThe prescriber may have meant one of these common regimens:\n- 400\u2013800 mg by mouth every 6\u20138 hours, up to 3,200 mg/day, or\n- 1600 mg total per day, split into several doses.\n\nI can enter the order once a prescriber confirms the intended dose and frequency. If the prescriber really does want the original dose, they should enter it themselves or give an explicit override with a clinical rationale."
      }
     ],
